  "explanation": "In C++, when a class contains virtual functions, the compiler implements dynamic dispatch through a vtable—a hidden table of function pointers associated with each polymorphic object. This vtable enables runtime resolution of function calls, allowing the program to invoke the correct overridden function based on the actual object's type, not the static type. Internally, each polymorphic object contains a pointer to its class's vtable, and function calls are dispatched by dereferencing this pointer and jumping to the appropriate function address. This mechanism is fundamental to supporting runtime polymorphism in the C++ object model, providing flexibility at the cost of additional memory overhead and indirection during function calls.\n\nIn C++, when a class contains virtual functions, the compiler creates a hidden pointer within each object instance pointing to a vtable—an array of function pointers. This vtable enables dynamic dispatch, allowing the program to invoke the correct function implementation at runtime based on the actual object's type, even when accessed through a base class pointer. Internally, the vtable mechanism involves compiler-generated tables, pointer adjustments, and specific calling conventions that facilitate polymorphic behavior while maintaining efficient memory layout and function call semantics.\n\nIn C++, when a class contains virtual functions, the compiler creates a vtable—a table of function pointers—that enables dynamic (runtime) dispatch of virtual functions. Each polymorphic object contains an implicit pointer to its class's vtable, allowing the program to invoke the correct function implementation based on the object's actual runtime type. This mechanism is fundamental to supporting runtime polymorphism, enabling the correct method to be called even when accessed through a base class pointer or reference.",
